  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/d/d5/Fantastic_Four_Vol_1_21.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Peace and quiet is disturbed at the Baxter Building after Ben reads the headlines about someone named the \"Hate Monger\" causing trouble all over the city, spreading a message of hate but being too smart to break any laws (and upset that if he \"whumped him one,\" <i>he'd </i>be the one arrested instead of the hate-spewing rabble rouser).\n\nThe FF decide to go for a walk to clear their thoughts but soon run into a crowd of people egged on by the Hate Monger himself, shouting out things like \"Down with all foreigners!&nbsp; Down with everybody who <i>disagrees</i> with us! Hail to the Hate-Monger!\"&nbsp; And they watch in horror as the crowd seems to turn on their foreign-born neighbors, despite the fact that they are citizens like they.\n\nIt's too much for the Fantastic Four, and they agree as one to try and stop it.&nbsp; But just as they prepare to step forward, the Hate-Monger bathes them with his \"all-powerful H-ray.\"&nbsp; The Hate-Monger is eager to see if it affects them as it has done with the others in the crowd.&nbsp; And indeed, in the next moments, the four immediately turn on each other.&nbsp; During the ensuing brawl, Johnny calls Sue \"the original Miss Phony of 1963!\"&nbsp; And Sue thinks she \"must have been <i>mad</i> to think I loved Reed Richards -- he's just a big <i>zero</i>!\"&nbsp; Reed even calls Ben \"fathead!\"&nbsp; And with that, the FF splits up!&nbsp; Hate Monger is thrilled!\n\nReed returns to the Baxter Building to find a familiar face - \"Sarge\" Nick Fury, an old army buddy, whom he hasn't seen \"since the end of the <i>war</i>.\"&nbsp; He's with the CIA now, and he's now a Colonel.&nbsp; As they talk, Fury mentions a scene from issue #3 of his own magazine, where Major Reed Richards makes an appearance!&nbsp; He warns Reed of unrest in a South American country where people have suddenly seemed to go nuts, and he's asking the FF to investigate, but Reed is done with the FF and heads off by himself in the pogo plane.&nbsp; Fury is so sure that the Hate Monger has affected him that he remains behind.\n\nBut the other three members see the plane flying away and all rush back to HQ to ensure that whatever Reed is doing, he won't get to hog all the glory himself.&nbsp; Fury is delighted they're all present and manipulates them into insisting they <i>all</i> head to San Gusto, with Fury providing transportation using his authority with the CIA.\n\nBut the Hate Monger also sees them depart and worries that his operation might be affected, so he heads that way too.\n\nReed arrives and begins to attack the rebels, who are, of course, already under the influence of the Hate Monger.&nbsp; The villain eventually <i>also</i> arrives, and before long, Richards is abducted, right after stumbling onto the large \"hate-ray machine\" that is presumably responsible for the nation's unrest.&nbsp; Hate Monger begins monologuing and explains his evil plan to blanket the world with his hate-ray.&nbsp; But Nick Fury arrives and orders Hate Monger at gunpoint to give Reed the antidote.\n\nReed manages to get the other three FF members to take antidote pills also, and before you know it, the FF is whole again and not hating each other!&nbsp; Working as a team again, they tackle the Hate Monger.&nbsp; Sue knocks his aim off as he attempts to hit Johnny with the H-ray again, and instead, strikes his own men by mistake, who begin to hate him and attack -- killing their former leader.\n\nFury and Reed lift the Hate Monger's mask and <i>gasp</i>!&nbsp; It's <i>Adolf Hitler</i>!&nbsp; \"The hate-ray must have been one of the last achievements of his enslaved Nazi scientists!\"&nbsp; But Reed isn't sure this is the real Hitler or merely \"one of the many <i>doubles</i> the Feuhrer was reported to have.\"\n\nThe day is saved, and they all head back home, leaving us with a final preachy morality speech:&nbsp; \"Until men truly love each other, regardless of race, creed or color, the Hate Monger will <i>still</i> be undefeated!&nbsp; Let's never forget that!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME -- write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME -- write something",
  "review_text": "[ASSET: left | HateMonger | width=12%] This issue introduced readers to the \"Hate-Monger,\" a venom-spouting villain whose entire existence seems to serve no other purpose than to stir up dissent in his followers, using an \"H-ray\" gun to activate hate so that they will go out and commit acts of violence against each other.\n\nSay what you want about his largely cheesy \"hate-ray,\" but it gave the co-creators an easy, built-in way to have the members of the FF go after each other (not that they don't do this <i>already</i> in every issue), which artist Jack Kirby got a [SPAN: severity=info; text=lot of mileage] out of in terms page count.\n[ASSET: center | TurnOnEachOther | width=67%]\n\nIt wasn't until the end of page 8 that the FF had settled down, but in the process (still affected by the sudden hatred toward each other), <b>disbanded</b>!\n\nOnce again, [SPAN: severity=warning; text=mission accomplished] in terms of chewing up real estate.&nbsp; It's simply <i>astonishing</i> how well Jack turns a simple concept into three pages of space-wasting mayhem.\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Okay, maybe \"wasting\" is a poor choice of words --I admit it!&nbsp; Jack is just so <i>good</i> at it!)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nBut with over a third of the book already behind them, Stan and Jack finally got around to bringing in the <i>real</i> guest star, one Sargent Nick Fury himself (though now \"Colonel\" Fury):\n[ASSET: center | EnterNickFury | width=67%]\n\nWe're told he's now with the CIA and has come to request aid from the Fantastic Four to lend an assist with a South America country named \"San Gusto,\" which the US government is trying to help move toward democracy.&nbsp; But something has recently gone haywire and sparked a full-scale revolution.\n\n[ASSET: right | FuryManipulation | width=12%] After the others see Reed take a pogo plane without them, I did at least like the way that Fury recognized that the FF all seemed to have been affected by the Hate-Monger's ray and actually <i>manipulated</i> the other three into heading toward the South American country, all so that all four of them would be together and likely be able to get to the bottom of what's going on down there.\n\n[DIVIDER]\nI got a kick out of the fact that, when Jack drew a scene where the Hate-Monger's \"sub-surface missile\" made its way toward South America on page 13, <i>entirely underground</i>, Stan took a moment to add an \"<i>Editor's Note</i>,\" explaining that this wasn't as ridiculous an idea as it appeared, noting that \"even now, the Russians are working on a similar vehicle...\"\n\nThis was no less than Stan saying, [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"You may <i>laugh, </i><b>but</b>...\"]\n[ASSET: center | NodToRealLife | width=67%]\n[SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(And indeed, I <i>did</i> laugh!&nbsp; Trying too hard, in my opinion...)]\n\nBut it does at least show that the Hate-Monger has <i>immense</i> wealth at his disposal (perhaps due to his manipulation of the South American country?).\n\n[DIVIDER]\nPages 15 and 16 focus on Reed's solo attempt to find out what's going on in San Gusto, and I have to admit he did a pretty good job of fending off the revolutionary invaders, all <i>without</i> the aid of his teammates.&nbsp; Jack found interesting ways for him to use his stretching abilities, even to the point of finding the large \"hate-ray machine\" and probe the ground beneath it, ultimately finding the underground chamber, where <i>alas</i>, he was ultimately detected and gassed.\n\n[ASSET: right | WahHoo | width=12%] The Hate-Monger then confronts him face-to-face, monologues his evil plan to [SPAN: severity=error; text=\"blanket the <i>world</i>\"] with similar rays and turn the population of the entire <i>globe</i> against one another.&nbsp; Things look bleak.\n[BR]\n[BR] That is... until the ageless Nick Fury arrives, gun blazing, and before long, demands that Reed be given the antidote.\n\nReed then spends a couple of pages tricking his teammates into <i>also</i> taking the antidote, all while Fury does... <i>well</i>... what he <i>always does</i>, which is to dive headfirst into a room filled with soldiers, guns and fists overwhelming everyone, fending off a horde of opponents... until the Fantastic Four arrive at last to help him polish things off.\n\n[ASSET: left | AimDisturbed | width=14%] Sue disrupts the villain's aim, and in the next moment, the Hate-Monger's own followers are hit, and they<i> turn on him</i>.&nbsp; Without so much as a [SOUNDEFFECT: text=BLAM!] sound effect later, [SPAN: severity=error; text=he is mowed down by their hatred] of their leader, and the only thing left to do is unmask the villain.\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=large; fonttype=bold-italic; text=GASP!&nbsp; It's Adolf Hitler!]\n\nBut Reed is quick to point out that the <i>real </i>Hitler was reported to have many doubles.\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Whether this is actually true or not, I don't know.)]\n\n[ASSET: right | DayIsSaved | width=10%] But with a couple of panels to spare...\n[BR]\n[BR] Stan can't stop himself from soliloquizing on the issue's final panel.\n[BR] Fury departs, leaving the FF to file the written report with the CIA.\n[BR] The FF are reunited, a more or less happy family.\n[BR]\n[BR] And once again, the [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=day is saved.]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | EvilPumpkin | width=12%] As for the artwork, I was taken aback at the appearance of the Human Torch on the splash page -- he looked like an evil pumpkin!\n[BR]\n[BR] In fact, the art in general seemed different.&nbsp; It was still unmistakably Jack Kirby, but the inker in this issue <i>sure</i> put his stamp on the images and made each of the FF look oddly different.&nbsp; I found it distracting and hope they don't use this inker again.\n\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nIn my mind, this was nothing less than outright cross-promotion of the new <b><i>Sgt. Fury and his Howling Commados</i></b> magazine, even mentioning Reed's own appearance in its issue 3 (which had been published a couple of months ago).\n\nTo be clear, I'm generally not against cross-promotion, and in this case, I found it nice to learn that Nick Fury was still around in the \"current day\" (that being 1963 at the time), promoted to colonel, and now working with the CIA.&nbsp; This is useful world-building, and I want to acknowledge it, even if the primary objective on the part of Marvel was to get more people interested in buying the World War II-oriented magazine.&nbsp; (But it won't work for me, as I outlined in my [PAGE: meanwhile] page.)\n\nAlso important to realize is the way that Stan and Jack didn't <i>dwell</i> on the WWII aspects too long.&nbsp; Fury came to Reed with a clear mission, and only when he noticed how Reed was filled with a surprising level of hatred toward his teammates did he step aside to investigate.\n\nI would be remiss in reminding 21<sup>st</sup> century readers that, while <i>we</i> may view World War II as ancient history, to the readers of the day, it was still very fresh in the minds of most adults, the war having only ended less than 20 years earlier at the time of publication.&nbsp; [LINK:1962:FF_11_ff1|FF #11|2026-05-01] already told us that both Reed and Ben were vets, having served in the war, and it doesn't hurt to remind people <i>now</i> that many of the original Marvel creators <i>themselves</i> had served in the war, including both Stan Lee and Jack Kirby.\n\nMy <i>point</i> is that the notion that Adolf Hitler might still have been alive in 1963 isn't as far-fetched as one might imagine, <i>certainly</i> no less far-fetched than the \"sub-surface missile\" that Stan and Jack tried to explain as plausible on pages 13 and 14 (and that made me laugh).&nbsp; And don't forget, Reed and Ben are still around, why not couldn't Nick Fury be as well?&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=This was a good way to be able to use Nick Fury in \"modern day\" stories.]\n\nTaken as a whole, we can certain raise an eyebrow (or maybe even <i>both</i>) at the Hate-Monger's access to vast wealth and question his global reach, but the story remained most readable from start to finish.\n\nThere were certainly silly elements, such as the villain's plan to blanket the entire world with a hate-ray.&nbsp; For the life of me, I cannot understand how anyone -- even a power-mad kook like the Hate-Monger -- would find it <i>beneficial</i> for the entire world to be hating on each other.\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=Exactly how does this help you in any way?]\n\nThere doesn't seem to be any logical answer advanced by the co-creators, who only seemed interested in plowing forward in order for the horrifying, final-page reveal.\n\nTo wrap up, it's fair to say that it had its moments, and I suppose the shock ending might have held more weight back in 1963 than it does now, but let's be honest:&nbsp; this one will never take a spot anywhere <i>near</i> the upper-echelon of Marvel stories, and I found that the addition of Nick Fury to the 1960s was far more important than the true identity of a misguided, rage-inducing, two-bit villain like the Hate-Monger.",
  "side_note": [
    "[HANG0] * Not that it affects the story at all, but when the FF go out for a walk, they <i>sure</i> get dressed up for the occasion.&nbsp; Page 4 shows that they changed out of the FF uniforms, and both Reed and Johnny are shown wearing jackets and ties.\n\n[HANG0] * The letters page includes an announcement that indicates the next issue will feature new powers for the Invisible Girl."
  ],
  "letters_page": "Mrs. P. Piccirillo indicates she's a \"53 year old grandmother of 13 grandchildren\" (<i>holy cow</i>,<i> she must have gotten started early</i>!)&nbsp; Marvel had previously posed the question as to who their oldest reader was, and she is declared the winner so far!\n\nMike Randal was complementary of Marvel's stance about reading in the letters page of [LINK:1963:FF_17|FF #17|2026-10-05] (I noted it in the August 1963 Monthly Summary), even if it meant reading their competitors' mags, getting the response of:&nbsp; <i>Aw, we don't mind doing it!&nbsp; Maybe someday someone will say something nice about US!</i>",
  "highlight": "FIXME -- write something"
}